{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>For you know, etc.</b> One needs to understand for what matter the verse gives the reason <b>כִּי אַתֶּם יְדַעְתֶּם</b>, and Ramban, of blessed memory, explained that it is the reason for what is said immediately afterward in proximity: <b>פֶּן יֵשׁ בָּכֶם</b> etc. — but it is somewhat far-fetched to place the reason before what he is about to say afterward. Perhaps the verse spoke in this order to call attention to one thing: since the verse required them to be included so as to obligate along with them also those who come after them, a person might say that the line of justice opposes this matter — for after all, one does not impose liability upon a person not in his presence (Ketubot 11a), and this acceptance that the fathers undertook upon the sons would be of no avail. Therefore He said <b>כִּי אַתֶּם יְדַעְתֶּם</b> and finishes <b>וַתִּרְאוּ</b> etc. — meaning, you saw that they are nothing but detestable and an abomination, and from now on you do no more than win for them the merit to distance them from the abominations of the nations and to choose a living Elohim, and you are able to obligate them just as you yourselves are obligated. And this is what he was precise about in calling them their detestable things and their idols — to say that they knew and understood their lowness and their repulsiveness."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:20:05.792290053Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}